          "message": "## Done — INI parser implemented, `moon test` green (42/42)\n\n### What I built\n\nA real, line-based INI parser in MoonBit, split by responsibility in the root `ini` package:\n\n| file | responsibility |\n| --- | --- |\n| `ini_error.mbt` | `pub suberror IniError(Int, String)` — line number + message for every rejection |\n| `text.mbt` | char classes (`is_blank`, `is_control`), `StringView` trimming, trailing-backslash run length |\n| `lines.mbt` | `Lines` cursor: LF/CRLF, CR of CRLF belongs to the break, no synthetic final empty line (so a dangling `\\` is detectable) |\n| `value.mbt` | carrier selection, quoted scanning, inline comments, backslash continuations, per-carrier escape decoding |\n| `parser.mbt` | line classification, `[name]` headers (quote-pair stripping), first `=`/`:` split, key validation, `parse` |\n| `printer.mbt` | `print`: globals first, every value through the double-quoted carrier, section-name quoting only when needed |\n| `ini_dialect_test.mbt` | 30 new black-box tests, one group per rule in `specs/ini.md` |\n\nNo lookup tables: the logic is a scanner + value parser + document assembly. Continuation is decided on the raw backslash run **before** escapes are decoded (that is the only order that keeps `k=a\\\\` and `k=a\\` distinguishable); quoted values close only on their own line; each carrier decodes its own escape set and keeps unknown sequences verbatim.\n\n### Verification actually run\n\n- `moon test` → **42 tests, 42 passed, 0 failed** (12 public + 30 added).\n- `moon check` → 0 errors; the only 2 warnings are pre-existing ones from provided files (`Doc` used unqualified inside `ini_pub_test.mbt`, and `moonbitlang/core/test` unused by the lib target in `moon.pkg`).\n- `moon fmt`, `moon info` run; `pkg.generated.mbti` regenerated.\n- Extra property runs beyond the public suite: 3000-case `parse(print(doc)) == doc` over generated docs, and **20000 random strings** asserting `parse` never traps and that anything it accepts prints back to an equal document.\n- Two independent audit subagents: no panic/OOB/infinite-loop path found (~130k adversarial inputs run in a lab copy); spec-rule-by-rule cross-check found no deviation from an explicit rule, only the underspecified choices listed below.\n\n### Judgement calls (documented in `README.md`)\n\nThe spec is silent on a few points; each choice lives in one function: continuation-line leading blanks are **kept** (\"appended where it left off\"); blanks after a marker are trimmed before the run is counted; an inline comment is stripped before the run count; a comment glued to `]` or a closing quote is accepted; `[\"\"]` is rejected (empty name after quote removal); a lone `\\r` is data, not a line break. `print` cannot express section names containing `[`/`]` or an empty name — outside the dialect and unreachable from `parse`, which is exactly the domain `ini_spec.mbt` promises.\n\n### Caveat to disclose\n\nTask instructions required running `moon fmt`; for the provided files it normalized CRLF→LF and applied two modernizations — `} derive(Eq, Debug)` → `#derive(Eq, Debug)` in `ini_spec.mbt`, and `Int` → `Int32` in two labelled parameters of `ini_qc.mbt`. Declarations, tests and semantics are unchanged (both are pure formatter migrations); the public test file and `specs/` only lost CR line endings. I did not hand-edit any protected file beyond that."
